# Patch 0004 - generated Swift async runtime: one owner for the raw future handle
#
# Upstream base: boltffi v0.30.1, tag commit 2e6320a6d92cb591d22b908477f3a47da7ebc9bc
# Upstream status: NO released, merged, or open-PR fix exists for this defect as of
#                  2026-09-16. `git diff v0.30.1 origin/main -- boltffi_backend/templates/target/swift/`
#                  is empty (main is 8e5f266a; the only Swift async changes since the
#                  tag are the pre-tag fixes 4de706e6/b01038ef/7c51c156), open PR #732
#                  does not touch the Swift backend, and issues #664/#778 are open.
#
# Prior art (independent derivation; no code copied): VikashLoomba/Portal
#   (8e55cc895b2dc3e1d1dcc6e4ff4840999f9a9ff1, no license file) fails closed with a
#   three-line Swift patch that stops the inline free; it has no lifetime object, so
#   nothing here is copied from it. UniFFI's `Async.swift` carries the opposite
#   reference design - the Rust future holds the foreign handle and calls back into
#   it - which is what this runtime has to emulate without a UniFFI-style object map.
#
# Invariant this patch establishes (stateable, testable, and false without it):
#   `rust_future_free(handle)` (a) happens exactly once per call, (b) never runs
#   while any poll, any native-delivered callback, or any queued re-poll can still
#   dereference the handle, and (c) runs before the caller is resumed, so the native
#   future cannot outlive the call that created it.
#
# Why (c) is part of it, measured not assumed: the v2 form of this patch deferred
#   the free to `deinit`, which is safe but *asynchronous* - the caller could be
#   resumed before the free ran. Executed evidence (run 35108329894, Apple job
#   104835220552, `BoltOwnership.repeatedCancellation`) recorded
#   `Expectation failed: (probe.active() -> 1) == 0` in 45 of 50 iterations: the
#   Rust body of a cancelled call was still alive when the cancelled call returned.
#   That is a real behavioural difference a caller can observe, so the terminal
#   paths now free on the call's own queue *before* resuming: `terminal(cancel:then:)`
#   for cancellation and completion, and `Owner.freeOnQueue()` (queue-confined,
#   idempotent) as the single free site.
#
# Why the free is not simply done inline, where the old runtime did it: the
#   completion or cancellation callback arrives *inside* `rust_future_poll` /
#   `rust_future_cancel`. Freeing there is a use-after-free the moment the runtime
#   touches the future again - and it does: `store_continuation` writes the new
#   continuation after invoking a displaced one, and `RustFuture::poll` keeps using
#   `&Arc<RustFuture>` for the rest of its frame. Executed RED evidence (run
#   35102072270, Apple job 104813721788, pre-0004 runtime) recorded exactly that:
#   `BOLT_LIFETIME completionInsidePollFrame=VIOLATION(free inside a native call;
#   free inside a native callback) ... frees=1` and `wakeDrivenRepoll=VIOLATION(...)
#   displacements=1`. So the free is queued behind the native call, never inside it.
#
# Test that fails if reverted: `host/LifetimeProbe.swift` (built by apple-proof.sh,
#   run against the generated runtime in the real Xcode test target) fails with
#   recorded violations on the pre-0004 generator - see
#   `scripts/fetch-patched-boltffi.sh --apply-only --exclude 0004` - and passes on
#   this one. The native acceptance suite additionally requires the real-Rust
#   cancellation tests (`BoltOwnership.repeatedCancellation`,
#   `cancellationRacingReadiness`) to observe the drained future.
#
# What breaks if wrong: the mock records "poll after free", "complete after free",
#   "cancel after free", "double free", "free inside a native call" and "free inside
#   a native callback", and every test asserts the recording is empty. A wrong
#   design fails visibly rather than corrupting memory silently.
#
# The one path that is not free-before-resume: if the generated completion closure
#   *throws*, `Error` is not `Sendable`, and boxing it in an `@unchecked Sendable`
#   wrapper is the escape hatch this candidate refuses. That path resumes from the
#   poll frame and lets `deinit` release the handle - still exactly once, still
#   never inside a native frame. It is recorded here rather than hidden.
#
